@defgroup micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-runners Test Runners
@brief The runner initialises the framework and returns the exit code.

@details
A test program has one runner, usually a global object, which `main()`
uses in three steps:
1. `initialise (argc, argv)` reads the command line options (see
   @ref micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cli); on platforms without a command
   line, `initialise (runner_options)` sets the options directly. The call
   is optional; without it, the default options are used;
2. `add_top_suite()` returns the top suite, on which `main()` creates its
   own test cases (see @ref micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-test-suites).
   The call is optional, for programs whose test cases are all in other
   suites;
3. `run_suites()` runs the other suites, writes the totals, and returns
   the exit code, which `main()` returns to the system.

The exit code is:
- 0 when all the checks passed, or when the test cases were only listed;
- 1 when a check failed, or when the program used the runner wrongly (for
  example, it added no suite at all);
- 2 when the command line had an error, or when the filters selected no
  test case.

@par Example

@code{.cpp}
#include <micro-os-plus/micro-test-plus.h>

namespace mt = micro_os_plus::micro_test_plus;

mt::runner tr;

int
main (int argc, char* argv[])
{
  tr.initialise (argc, argv);

  auto& ts = tr.add_top_suite ("Minimal");

  ts.test ("Check truth", [] (auto& t) {
    t.expect (true);
  });

  return tr.run_suites ();
}
@endcode

The program writes (the passed checks are displayed only with the
`--verbose` option):

@code{.console}
# Running: minimal
# Built with ...
TAP version 14
# Subtest: Minimal
    ok 1 - Check truth # { passed, 1 check }

    1..1
ok 1 - Minimal # { passed, 1 check in 1 test case, time: 0.039 ms }

1..1
# { total: 1 check passed, 0 failed, in 1 test case, 1 test suite, time: 0.074 ms }
@endcode

The name of the top suite is optional. Without it, the suite is named
after the program, the file name of `argv[0]` without its extension
(`minimal` above); without `argv[0]`, it is named `default suite`.
